AEM Forms – PDF Forms, HTML Forms & Adaptive Forms

1. What is AEM Forms?

Adobe Experience Manager (AEM) Forms is used to create, manage, publish, and process digital forms. It helps collect user information and integrate form submissions with backend systems.

2. Types of AEM Forms

1. PDF Forms

- PDF stands for Portable Document Format.
- Used to collect information through fillable PDF documents.
- Users can fill out supported PDF forms and save them locally.
- Useful for applications, agreements, and official documents.
- Interactive PDF forms can support digital signatures.

Example: A student downloads a college admission PDF, fills in personal details, and submits it.

2. HTML Forms

- HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language.
- Forms are displayed in a web browser.
- Users can access them through a website on desktop or mobile devices.
- Useful for browser-based data collection.

Example: A student enters their name, email, and department in an online registration form.

3. Adaptive Forms

- Adaptive Forms are responsive and dynamic forms.
- They adjust their layout to different screen sizes.
- They can show or hide fields based on user input.
- They support validation and configurable submission actions.
- They can integrate with backend services and data sources.

Example: When a user selects “Other” as their department, an additional text field appears to enter the department name.

3. Comparison

Feature| PDF Form| HTML Form| Adaptive Form
Format| PDF document| Browser-based HTML| Responsive web form
Offline use| Possible, depending on form| Usually requires a website| Usually requires a website
Responsive layout| Limited| Depends on implementation| Built for adaptive layouts
Dynamic fields| Supported in some PDF forms| Possible with development| Supported through rules and configuration
Example| Application document| Registration page| Dynamic online application

4. Real-Time Use Cases

- PDF Forms: Agreements, printable applications, official records.
- HTML Forms: Contact forms, feedback forms, simple registration pages.
- Adaptive Forms: Loan applications, insurance claims, employee onboarding, and online applications.

5. Important Viva Questions

Q1. What is AEM Forms?

AEM Forms is an Adobe solution for creating, managing, publishing, and processing digital forms.

Q2. What is the difference between PDF Forms and Adaptive Forms?

PDF Forms are document-based, whereas Adaptive Forms are responsive web forms that can dynamically change according to user input.

Q3. Why are Adaptive Forms useful?

They provide responsive layouts, validation, conditional fields, and configurable submission actions.

Q4. Can HTML Forms work on mobile devices?

Yes. HTML Forms can be accessed on mobile devices, and their responsiveness depends on the implementation.

Q5. Can Adaptive Forms connect to a database?

Yes. They can integrate with configured data sources through a Form Data Model or other supported integration methods.

Conclusion

AEM Forms provides different ways to collect information. PDF Forms are useful for document-based workflows, HTML Forms are useful for browser-based data collection, and Adaptive Forms are useful for responsive, dynamic digital experiences.

